1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,750
Wee Bin, the Shipping Magnate
Whose Firm Outlived Its Own Name

2
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:08,662
In March 1911, the Straits
Times announced the end

3
00:00:08,742 --> 00:00:12,575
of one of Singapore's
oldest Chinese businesses.

4
00:00:12,575 --> 00:00:16,062
"Steps are in progress," it
reported, "to wind up the

5
00:00:16,142 --> 00:00:19,575
well-known and long-established
business of Messrs.

6
00:00:19,575 --> 00:00:23,574
Wee Bin and Co., of 106
Market St." Three weeks later

7
00:00:23,654 --> 00:00:27,422
the firm's steamers were
sold by public auction on

8
00:00:27,502 --> 00:00:31,350
the waterfront they had
served for half a century.

9
00:00:31,350 --> 00:00:36,125
The strange part of the story is
the date on the founder's grave.

10
00:00:36,125 --> 00:00:38,384
Wee Bin had died in 1868,

11
00:00:38,464 --> 00:00:41,925
forty-three years before the auction.

12
00:00:41,925 --> 00:00:46,026
In between, his firm — at its
height the largest Chinese shipping

13
00:00:46,106 --> 00:00:50,207
business in Singapore, with a
fleet of more than twenty vessels —

14
00:00:50,287 --> 00:00:54,516
was carried on by his son, who died
at thirty-six, by his grandson,

15
00:00:54,596 --> 00:00:58,182
who died at twenty-six, and
above all by a son-in-law who

16
00:00:58,262 --> 00:01:02,250
had joined the firm as a boy
helping its cook buy ingredients.

17
00:01:02,250 --> 00:01:06,844
Wee Bin was a Hokkien, born
in 1823 in Longxi county,

18
00:01:06,924 --> 00:01:10,275
in the Zhangzhou prefecture of Fujian.

19
00:01:10,275 --> 00:01:15,200
No record found for this post
says when he arrived in Singapore.

20
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:20,856
By 1856, aged thirty-three, he was
established enough to found his own house:

21
00:01:20,936 --> 00:01:24,803
Wee Bin & Co., trading under
the chop, or trade name,

22
00:01:24,883 --> 00:01:29,725
Hong Guan, in Market Street, a
short walk from the river godowns.

23
00:01:29,725 --> 00:01:33,004
He ran it, unusually
for its time and place,

24
00:01:33,084 --> 00:01:35,375
on Western business practices.

25
00:01:35,375 --> 00:01:39,825
The firm's first connections
were with the Dutch East Indies.

26
00:01:39,825 --> 00:01:43,809
It built up trade with houses
in Bali until it had become

27
00:01:43,889 --> 00:01:47,525
the greatest importer of
products from that island.

28
00:01:47,525 --> 00:01:51,720
It traded in all kinds of
earthenware, it manufactured sago,

29
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:56,075
it acted as merchant bankers,
and above all it bought ships.

30
00:01:56,075 --> 00:01:59,594
By the end of Wee Bin's
life the firm had built up a

31
00:01:59,674 --> 00:02:04,163
fleet of over twenty vessels for
the China and Dutch Indies trade,

32
00:02:04,243 --> 00:02:07,150
with branches in the Indies to serve them.

33
00:02:07,150 --> 00:02:10,693
In an 1866 survey, fifty-six
of the a hundred and

34
00:02:10,773 --> 00:02:15,498
seventy-eight vessels registered
in Singapore were Chinese-owned,

35
00:02:15,578 --> 00:02:19,275
and Wee Bin & Co. stood
at the head of that world.

36
00:02:19,275 --> 00:02:23,173
Something of the man's standing
can be seen in the small

37
00:02:23,253 --> 00:02:27,933
print of the newspapers: in 1865,
a decade after founding his firm,

38
00:02:28,013 --> 00:02:31,201
Wee Bin appears as sole
executor to the estate

39
00:02:31,281 --> 00:02:34,753
of a fellow merchant — the
kind of trust merchants

40
00:02:34,833 --> 00:02:39,025
placed only in one of their
own number whose word was good.

41
00:02:39,025 --> 00:02:40,900
He married twice.

42
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:44,204
One of his wives was a
daughter of Kiong Kong Tuan,

43
00:02:44,284 --> 00:02:48,517
the Penang merchant who held the
colony's opium and spirit farms;

44
00:02:48,597 --> 00:02:53,360
Wee Bin's own road to wealth was ships,
not farms, though he briefly went

45
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:57,341
into partnership with his
brother-in-law Kiong Seok Wee in a

46
00:02:57,421 --> 00:03:01,800
ship-chandlery and a general trading
firm alongside his own house.

47
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:05,712
He died in 1868, aged
forty-five, leaving a son,

48
00:03:05,792 --> 00:03:09,950
Wee Boon Teck, still in
his teens, and a daughter.

49
00:03:09,950 --> 00:03:14,625
The surprise of the story is that
the firm did not die with him.

50
00:03:14,625 --> 00:03:17,975
It prospered for another
forty-three years.

51
00:03:17,975 --> 00:03:22,457
Boon Teck grew into the business
and, in Song Ong Siang's words,

52
00:03:22,538 --> 00:03:27,100
"materially improved and strengthened
the position of the firm".

53
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:30,738
He sat on the committees of
the Tan Tock Seng Hospital and

54
00:03:30,818 --> 00:03:34,648
the Po Leung Kuk, the society
that protected women and girls,

55
00:03:34,728 --> 00:03:38,046
and he was remembered for
his charity: a gift of four

56
00:03:38,126 --> 00:03:42,020
thousand dollars to the Tan Tock
Seng Hospital was held by the

57
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:45,674
government for some twenty
years and then spent on a ward

58
00:03:45,754 --> 00:03:49,600
in the hospital's Moulmein Road
building that bore his name.

59
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:54,448
He died on the 22nd of September
1888, aged thirty-six —

60
00:03:54,528 --> 00:03:58,400
the second towkay of
Hong Guan to die young.

61
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:02,998
His son, Wee Siang Tat, became
a partner in January 1900,

62
00:04:03,078 --> 00:04:05,950
and the succession thinned further.

63
00:04:05,950 --> 00:04:09,790
Siang Tat had no head for
business; he was a music lover,

64
00:04:09,870 --> 00:04:12,817
one of a dozen young
Straits Chinese who met

65
00:04:12,897 --> 00:04:16,050
regularly to practise
the violin at Siam House,

66
00:04:16,130 --> 00:04:20,050
the North Bridge Road home of
the merchant Tan Kim Ching.

67
00:04:20,050 --> 00:04:24,218
He died fourteen months
after becoming a partner,

68
00:04:24,298 --> 00:04:28,025
on the 14th of March
1901, aged twenty-six.

69
00:04:28,025 --> 00:04:32,200
By then the firm's survival
rested on the son-in-law.

70
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:37,443
Lim Ho Puah had been born in Amoy
in 1841 and came to Singapore young,

71
00:04:37,523 --> 00:04:42,082
entering Wee Bin & Co. in the
humblest capacity in the house:

72
00:04:42,162 --> 00:04:46,725
as an apprentice who helped the
firm's cook buy ingredients.

73
00:04:46,725 --> 00:04:50,087
His head for accounts
carried him up to bookkeeper,

74
00:04:50,167 --> 00:04:53,123
and his industry so
impressed the towkay that

75
00:04:53,203 --> 00:04:56,375
Wee Bin gave him his only
daughter in marriage.

76
00:04:56,375 --> 00:05:00,354
After Boon Teck's death
in 1888, Lim, as executor,

77
00:05:00,434 --> 00:05:04,250
took over the management
of the shipping fleet.

78
00:05:04,250 --> 00:05:08,575
The kitchen boy had become
the firm's senior partner.

79
00:05:08,575 --> 00:05:11,690
Under Lim the firm
reached beyond the Indies

80
00:05:11,770 --> 00:05:14,675
trade into the China passenger business.

81
00:05:14,675 --> 00:05:18,550
It entered that trade in 1891.

82
00:05:18,550 --> 00:05:24,125
In 1894 Lim bought a new steamer
and renamed her Hong Leong.

83
00:05:24,125 --> 00:05:27,956
In 1896 he bought the Glen
Line steamer Glenfalloch,

84
00:05:28,036 --> 00:05:32,925
then the largest ship in the trade
between the Straits and China.

85
00:05:32,925 --> 00:05:36,236
In 1901 he took delivery of the Hong Moh,

86
00:05:36,316 --> 00:05:40,700
a two-funnelled steamer of
nearly four thousand tons.

87
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:43,881
With their sister ships
they ran a weekly service —

88
00:05:43,961 --> 00:05:47,270
Rangoon, Penang, Singapore,
Hong Kong, Swatow, Amoy —

89
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:51,746
and a missionary who knew the harbour
described the firm's house flag,

90
00:05:51,826 --> 00:05:53,600
a blue flag with a red cross,

91
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:57,900
"flying from the mastheads of quite
a fleet of coasting steamers".

92
00:05:57,900 --> 00:06:02,053
Lim's standing outside the firm
matched it: a director of the

93
00:06:02,133 --> 00:06:06,703
Tanjong Pagar Dock Company, a member
of the Chinese Advisory Board,

94
00:06:06,783 --> 00:06:11,225
a committee man of the Po Leung
Kuk, and a Justice of the Peace.

95
00:06:11,225 --> 00:06:15,368
When Wee Siang Tat died in
1901, Lim Ho Puah became the

96
00:06:15,448 --> 00:06:19,821
sole surviving partner of Wee
Bin & Co. The firm traded on

97
00:06:19,901 --> 00:06:24,200
for another decade, but its
ownership was quietly stuck.

98
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:28,600
Siang Tat's widow, Ho Shiok Chui
Neo, was an heir to the estate;

99
00:06:28,680 --> 00:06:33,650
while she remained a widow, the old
partnership could rest undisturbed.

100
00:06:33,650 --> 00:06:39,225
In March 1909 she remarried, and
the estate had to be divided.

101
00:06:39,225 --> 00:06:43,613
In March 1910 the family's property
— ninety-seven lots of it —

102
00:06:43,693 --> 00:06:48,800
went under the hammer and realised
almost four hundred thousand dollars.

103
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:52,475
What remained was to
wind up the firm itself.

104
00:06:52,475 --> 00:06:57,875
It is worth being clear about
what the 1911 liquidation was not.

105
00:06:57,875 --> 00:07:01,775
It was not a bankruptcy,
and it was not a failure.

106
00:07:01,775 --> 00:07:05,564
It was a seventy-year-old
man settling a family estate

107
00:07:05,644 --> 00:07:09,800
that the law of partnership
would no longer let him carry.

108
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:12,771
The Straits Times said
as much in its own way,

109
00:07:12,851 --> 00:07:18,025
calling the concern "well-known and
long-established" as it announced the end.

110
00:07:18,025 --> 00:07:22,200
On the 28th of March 1911, Messrs.

111
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:26,150
Powell & Co. sold the
steamers by public auction.

112
00:07:26,150 --> 00:07:29,989
The Pinang Gazette printed
the prices: the Glenfalloch at

113
00:07:30,069 --> 00:07:34,400
sixty thousand dollars, the Hong
Wan I at eighty-five thousand.

114
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:38,508
Two of the smaller ships went
to the agent of the Java sugar

115
00:07:38,588 --> 00:07:42,775
king Oei Tiong Ham; one or two
others went to third parties.

116
00:07:42,775 --> 00:07:46,215
And the large steamers at
the heart of the fleet were

117
00:07:46,295 --> 00:07:49,868
bought by the man who had
the strongest claim to them —

118
00:07:49,948 --> 00:07:54,000
Lim Peng Siang, Lim Ho Puah's
son and Wee Bin's own grandson.

119
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,641
Readers of our post on Lim Peng Siang

120
00:07:56,721 --> 00:07:59,075
will know the rest of his story.

121
00:07:59,075 --> 00:08:03,423
In 1904, on a loan of fifty
thousand dollars from his father,

122
00:08:03,503 --> 00:08:07,705
he and his brother Lim Peng
Mau had founded their own firm,

123
00:08:07,785 --> 00:08:11,850
Ho Hong Co., and gone into
rice milling and coconut oil.

124
00:08:11,850 --> 00:08:16,000
The name they chose deserves
a moment's attention.

125
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:19,419
Ho Hong joins "Ho", from
Lim Ho Puah, to "Hong",

126
00:08:19,499 --> 00:08:21,751
from Hong Guan, Wee Bin's chop —

127
00:08:21,831 --> 00:08:26,350
the successor firm carried the
old firm's name inside its own.

128
00:08:26,350 --> 00:08:31,278
The steamers bought in 1911 became
Ho Hong's shipping department,

129
00:08:31,358 --> 00:08:36,131
and on the 27th of May 1913 they
were formalised as the Ho Hong

130
00:08:36,211 --> 00:08:41,450
Steamship Co. Limited, with five
million dollars in nominal capital.

131
00:08:41,450 --> 00:08:45,255
The Glenfalloch, the Hong
Bee and the Hong Wan I were

132
00:08:45,335 --> 00:08:50,019
transferred into its ownership
within days, the Hong Moh followed

133
00:08:50,099 --> 00:08:54,637
in January 1914, and by 1926 the
fleet ran to fourteen vessels,

134
00:08:54,717 --> 00:08:58,675
some of them named, fittingly,
after Singapore places.

135
00:08:58,675 --> 00:09:02,285
Within a year of that last
transfer, the man who had

136
00:09:02,365 --> 00:09:06,125
held the old firm together
for four decades was dead.

137
00:09:06,125 --> 00:09:10,375
Lim Ho Puah died in February 1914.

138
00:09:10,375 --> 00:09:14,727
His funeral procession, on
Sunday the 15th of March 1914,

139
00:09:14,807 --> 00:09:19,780
was reported as the longest ever
seen in Singapore, as his coffin

140
00:09:19,860 --> 00:09:24,525
was carried to the water to be
embarked for burial in China.

141
00:09:24,525 --> 00:09:28,397
It did not pass without
incident: the silken cover draped

142
00:09:28,477 --> 00:09:31,932
over the coffin was stolen
along the way, and a man

143
00:09:32,012 --> 00:09:35,825
was later convicted in court
of dishonestly keeping it.

144
00:09:35,825 --> 00:09:40,099
The Singapore Free Press titled
its report "The Coffin Cover —

145
00:09:40,179 --> 00:09:44,945
an incident of Singapore's longest
funeral", and that small theft is,

146
00:09:45,025 --> 00:09:49,450
in its way, the last newspaper
trace of the house of Hong Guan.

147
00:09:49,450 --> 00:09:53,325
Nothing on Market Street
today marks the firm.

148
00:09:53,325 --> 00:09:58,420
The name Wee Bin survives in one
portrait, in Song Ong Siang's pages,

149
00:09:58,500 --> 00:10:02,325
and in the shipping
intelligence of old newspapers.

150
00:10:02,325 --> 00:10:06,041
But the trade itself never
stopped: it passed, ships,

151
00:10:06,121 --> 00:10:09,765
routes and all, into a
grandson's company whose name

152
00:10:09,845 --> 00:10:12,558
still held a syllable of the old chop —

153
00:10:12,638 --> 00:10:17,150
and through Ho Hong's bank, in
time, into the ancestry of OCBC.

154
00:10:17,150 --> 00:10:21,325
The firm outlived its
founder by forty-three years.

155
00:10:21,325 --> 00:10:25,300
Then, in everything but
name, it outlived itself.

156
00:10:25,300 --> 00:10:28,990
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Wee Bin & Co. was one

157
00:10:29,070 --> 00:10:33,164
of the first great Chinese-owned
shipping houses of Singapore,

158
00:10:33,244 --> 00:10:36,665
built in the founder's
twelve working years and then

159
00:10:36,745 --> 00:10:40,165
sustained for four decades
by family succession of a

160
00:10:40,245 --> 00:10:44,150
kind that rarely survives one
generation, let alone three.

161
00:10:44,150 --> 00:10:46,826
Its end was not collapse but settlement —

162
00:10:46,906 --> 00:10:51,463
and its absorption into Ho Hong
connects the age of the Market Street

163
00:10:51,543 --> 00:10:55,092
godowns to the industrial
groups, and the bank merger,

164
00:10:55,172 --> 00:10:58,600
that shaped the Singapore
of the twentieth century.

165
00:10:58,600 --> 00:11:03,254
It is also the family prequel to
our post on the founder's grandson,

166
00:11:03,334 --> 00:11:07,650
Lim Peng Siang, the man Singapore
called its greatest magnate.
